#{[17592186455436 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455436 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76584" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455436 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455436 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{3}\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455436 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "22016566-6911-5c05-b305-606b27ad2ee0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455436 #edb/ref 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt{3})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455452 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455452 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76582" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455452 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455452 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455452 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "50247cc3-8637-5b67-95c3-4cdd3dd3329e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455452 #edb/ref 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455490 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455490 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76609" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455490 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455490 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}xy(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=1\\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455490 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bcf9f8c6-5d3c-5211-90e2-bddb94921272" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455490 #edb/ref 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $-\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=xy(1-{x}^{2})$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455497 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455497 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76607" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455497 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455497 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455497 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ce434154-3398-5488-977f-b3adb13e5b25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455497 #edb/ref 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186483435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186483445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186483447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186483462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186483476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1045 "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions Over Polar Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186483447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5612666d-368d-549d-8c98-b899d091fd25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482817 #edb/ref